<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/6th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Tricamarum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/533">533</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandalic-war">Vandalic War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Tricamarum was fought in December 533 west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, where the Byzantine army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> defeated the Vandal king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelimer">Gelimer</a> and his brother Tzazon, who was killed. Gelimer fled to the mountains and surrendered in 534. The battle ended the Vandal Kingdom and restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the Byzantine victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ad-decimum">Battle of Ad Decimum</a> on 13 September 533, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelimer">Gelimer</a> fell back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulla-regia">Bulla Regia</a> in Numidia. He recalled his brother Tzazo from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, where Tzazo had restored Vandal rule, offered the local Punic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> peoples a reward for every Byzantine head, and sent agents to win over the people of Carthage and the Hun mercenaries of Belisarius. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, fearing they would be kept in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, chose to wait and join the winning side. Early in December the reunited brothers marched on Carthage and cut the aqueduct that supplied most of its water.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>With the city walls not yet repaired and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> unreliable, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> chose to force a battle. He sent John the Armenian ahead with most of the cavalry to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelimer">Gelimer</a>'s camp by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medjerda">Medjerda River</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> had gathered their wives, children and property. On 15 December the Vandals stood behind a small stream, Tzazo with about 5,000 picked men in the centre and Moorish allies in reserve; the historian Ilkka Syvänne puts their whole force at 15,000–20,000. Belisarius faced them with 8,000–11,000 cavalry, his infantry still a day's march behind.</p><p>Tzazo's men threw back John's first two attacks. In the third nearly all the Byzantine cavalry of the centre closed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, Tzazo and several nobles were killed, and the line collapsed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> now joined the pursuit and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelimer">Gelimer</a> fled. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> lost fewer than 50 men and the Vandals about 800. When the camp was stormed, the soldiers broke ranks to plunder it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> needed a day to restore order.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a> opened its gates and the royal treasure was taken. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelimer">Gelimer</a>, deserted by his nobles, tried to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, was cut off, and took refuge with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a> in the mountains, where Pharas the Herulian besieged him through a hard winter. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearic Islands</a> passed to Justinian. Gelimer was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a>'s triumph, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> records him repeating, Vanities of vanities, all vanities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> became the base for the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Tricamarum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 533 Belisarius beat the Vandals at the Battle of Ad Decimum and the <b>Battle of Tricamarum</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
